Capture of iodide from wastewater by effective adsorptive membrane synthesized from MIL-125-NH\(_2\) and cross-linked chitosan
Mahmoud El-Shahat\(^{a}\), Ahmed E. Abdelhamid\(^{b}\), Reda M. Abdelhameed\(^{c,\ast}\)
\(^{a}\) Photochemistry Department, Chemical Industries Research Division, National Research Centre, Scopus affiliation ID 60014618, 33 EL Buhouth St., Dokki, Giza 12311, Egypt  \(^{b}\) Polymers & Pigments Department, National Research Centre, Scopus affiliation ID 60014618, 33 EL Buhouth St., Dokki, Giza 12622, Egypt  \(^{c}\) Applied Organic Chemistry Department, National Research Centre, Scopus affiliation ID 60014618, 33 EL Buhouth St., Dokki, Giza, 12622, Egypt
## A R T I C L E I N F O
## A B S T R A C T
Keywords:  Chitosan  Titanium based metal-organic frameworks  Epichlorohydrine  Iodine  Adsorption property
The mainly nuclear waste is radioactive iodine \((^{129}\mathrm{I}\,\mathrm{or}^{131}\mathrm{I})\) so its disposal have great significance to produce safe water. Porous metal-organic works (MOFs) have awesome potential as high-effective adsorbents for water treatment. In any case, destitute separation limits their viable application. All in all, we worked on the development of MOFs with cross linked chitosan as a promising adsorbent material for the remediation of environmental water polluted by iodine. Due to the drawbacks of current adsorbents such as low uptake capacity, high cost, and non-recyclability; a novel cross-linked chitosan-MOF composite was synthesized and used in the adsorptive removal of iodine from wastewater. The novel composite shows high iodine removal capacity \(399.68\,\mathrm{mg}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at room temperature. The cross-linked chitosan-MOF composite shows chemical and thermal stability, high removal efficiency for capture of iodine from wastewater and furthermore good recyclability.
## 1. Introduction
Water contamination with radioactive iodine has been occurred by mainly two sources. The first is atomic reactors because they depend on the fission of uranium atoms, including the production of radioactive iodine as a byproduct material (Li et al., 2017). The radioactive iodine was released to the environment from operating nuclear reactors (Council, 2004). The second source of radioactive iodine-contaminated wastewater is hospitals because they using radioactive isotopes for diagnostic and therapeutic applications with large amounts (Khan et al., 2010).
Chitosan is a recognized polysaccharide that can act as an adsorbent material in water treatment. Chitosan can efficiently adsorb dyes and heavy metals from water (Crini & Badot, 2008; Gerente, Lee, Cloirec, & McKay, 2007). It is well known that chitosan is the most suitable polymer for removal of iodine from wastewater (Zhang, Li, Mao, & He, 2013). Recently, chitosan microspheres display as the effective adsorbent for iodine removal (Zhang et al., 2019).
Metal organic framework is a combination between ions and organic linker (Rowsell & Yaghi, 2004). It has much advantage due to its high surface area and structure diversity (Long & Yaghi, 2009). MOFs have application in a lot of filed like gas storage (Chen et al., 2006) and separation(Abdelhameed, Abdel-Gawad, Taha, & Hegazi, 2017),
Metal organic framework is a combination between ions and organic linker (Rowsell & Yaghi, 2004). It has much advantage due to its high surface area and structure diversity (Long & Yaghi, 2009). MOFs have application in a lot of filed like gas storage (Chen et al., 2006) and separation(Abdelhameed, Abdel-Gawad, Taha, & Hegazi, 2017), catalysis (Abdelhameed & El-Shahat, 2019). The more interesting field is using MOFs in water treatment; recently MOFs were used in removal of pesticide (Abdel-Gawad, Afifi, Abdel-Hameed, & Hegazi, 2008; Abdel-Gawad, Abdelhameed, Elmesalamy, & Hegazi, 2011; Abdelhameed, Abdel-Gawad, Silva et al., 2017), heavy metals (Abdelhameed et al., 2019) and pharmaceutical intermediate (Abdelhameed & Emam, 2019). However using of MOFs for removal of iodine is rare. Bi (III) ions were doped onto mesoporous Fe-based MOFs (MIL-101(Fe)) producing Bi@MIL. The 15 % Bi@MIL composites shown quick iodine adsorption rate and the adsorption balance were accomplished in 20 min. The maximum adsorption capacities of iodine in the presence of 15 % Bi@MIL reach 189.6 mg g\(^{-1}\) (Xu, Zhang, Kang, & Li, 2019). Moreover; Ag (I) was loaded into MIL-101(Cr)-SO\(_3\)H; the resulted composite (MIL-101(Cr)-SO\(_3\)Ag) was applied to uptake of iodine from water. The adsorption uptake of iodine onto MIL-101(Cr)-SO\(_3\)Ag was 244.2 mg g\(^{-1}\), it is much higher than MIL-101(Cr)-SO\(_3\)H (94.1 mg g\(^{-1}\)) (Zhao et al., 2015). Additionally; Nano Ag\(_2\)O-Ag\(_2\)O\(_3\) as mixed-valence silver oxide was added to zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8). The composite (Ag\(_2\)O-Ag\(_2\)O\(_3\)@ZIF-8) connected for iodide take-up from wastewater. The adsorption capacity expanded by expanding Ag doped ratio, and the highest adsorption capacity for 20 %-Ag\(_2\)O-Ag\(_2\)O\(_3\)@ZIF-8 was 232.12 mg g\(^{-1}\) (Chen et al., 2019). The Ag (II) ions were interacting with fluorine in the framework of MIL-101
producing good adsorbents for capture of iodine. The exits composite within the cages of MIL-101 has an adsorption capacity of \(0.17\,\mathrm{mmol/L}\). The more important notification is increases iodine adsorption capacity with increasing the doping amount of silver onto Ag@MIL-101 composite (Mao et al., 2016).
Magnetic bio-metal-organic frameworks \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{\textcircled{\textcent}}\) Bio-MOF) were coated with folic acid (FA) and chitosan (CS). The \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{\textcircled{\textcent}}\) Bio-MOF/ FA/CS shows effective agent for anti tumor (Nejadshafiee et al., 2019). Bio-MOF coated with a mono dispersed layer of chitosan was synthesized and connected as a pH-responsive and target-selective framework for conveyance of doxorubicin (DOX) (Abazari et al., 2018). IRMOF-3, Folic acid, chitosan, magnetic nanoparticles and doxorubicin (DOX) were joined together and the composite show high DOX stacking
\((1.63\,\mathrm{g.g^{-1}})\) (Chowdhuri, Singh, Ghosh, & Sahu, 2016). Copper-based MOF \(\mathrm{[Cu_{4}C l]_{3}-(B T T r i)_{8}}\) ; \(\mathrm{H3BTTr}\dot{\mathrm{I}}=1,3,5\) -tris(1H-1,2,3-triazol-5-yl) benzene)] was consolidated inside chitosan to make films. The films were assessed for their capacity to improve NO release from S-nitrosoglutathione (Neufeld, Lutzke, Tapia, & Reynolds, 2017).
MIL-53(Al)/CS was tested as an adsorbent matrix in ultrasound-assisted solid-phase extraction (UA-SPE). It was applied for test of five parabens (methylparaben, ethylparaben, propylparaben, butylparaben, benzylparaben) in water samples (Li, Jia, Guo, & Hou, 2018). MIL-68(Al)/CS composite was applied to fast solid phase extraction (SPE) of trace levels of Pb(II) and Cd(II) (Asiabi, Mehdinia, & Jabbari, 2017). MIL-101(Fe)/CS composite were applied for mat-based extraction of trace amount of \(\Delta9\)-tetrahydrocannabinol (THC) from human whole
blood sample (Asiabi, Mehdinia, & Jabbari, 2017). Chitosan impregnated sod-ZMOF (sod-ZMOF/CS) was evaluated for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption using a packed bed column using a gas mixture containing \(15\ \%\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(85\ \%\) \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) . Sod-ZMOF/CS reached the best adsorption capacity at \(978\,\mathrm{mg}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (Singo, Molepo, Oluwasina, & Daramola, 2017).
Cu-hemin frameworks/CS/reduced graphene oxide (rGO) nanocomposite with interesting peroxidase-like bioactivity and great electrical conductivity was arranged for electrochemical \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) detecting displayed wide straight extend and low detection limit (Wang, Yang et al., 2016). The covalent modification of \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}\) (btc = benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylic acid) with CS and the electrochemically reduced graphene oxide (ERGO) was added to create hybrid film modified electrode, The MOF-based electrode is connected as an electrochemical detecting stage for the synchronous discovery of dihydroxybenzene isomers (DBIs) of hydroquinone (HQ), resorcinol (RS), and catechol (CT) (Yang, Wang, Qiu, Guo, & Gao, 2016).
MIL-125/CS was utilized to uptake Pb(II) from wastewater, the maximum adsorption uptake of \(\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{II})\) onto the MIL-125/CS reached up to \(407.50\,\mathrm{mg}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (Liang et al., 2018). MIL-101(Cr)/sodium alginate (MIL-101(Cr)/SA) and MIL-101(Cr)/CS composite were arranged and connected in adsorption of benzoic acid (BEN), ibuprofen (IBU) and ketoprofen (KET). The MIL-101(Cr)/CS display higher adsorption capacity than SA, CS and MIL-101(Cr)/SA (Zhuo et al., 2017). Chitosan / \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) , MIL-101 \(\mathrm{([Cr_{3}F(H_{2}O)_{4}O(bdc)_{3}}\) ; \(\mathrm{bdc}=1,4\) benzenedicarboxylate] and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{MIL}{-}101/\mathrm{CS}\) were prepared for the adsorptive removal of a harmful anionic methyl orange (MO) dye from aqueous solutions. The access of chitosan could clearly increase the adsorption capacity of MO onto the framework by about \(40\;\%\) in comparison with pure MIL-101(Liu, Ge, Yang, Jiang, & Qiu, 2016). Moreover, UiO-66/CS
composite shows the maximum adsorption capacity 93.6, 50.6, \(60\,\mathrm{mg}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for removal of \(\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})\) , \(\mathrm{{Cu}(I I)}\) and Ni(II), respectively, from water (Wang, Tao, Xu, & Yin, 2016). \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-101(Cr) and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL101(Al) could be homogeneously scattered in the polymeric matrix of chitosan. The positively charged MOF/chitosan composites were able to reject up to \(93.0\,\%\) of \(\mathrm{MgCl}_{2}\) , and the salt rejection followed the order of \(\mathrm{MgCl_{2}>C a C l_{2}>N a C l\;>\;N a_{2}S O_{4}}\) (Ma, Yang, Yao, Xu, & Tang, 2017).
The practical implementation of MOFs in removal of iodine typically requires incorporation within a polymer support, yet this immobilization has been shown to impair the ability of the MOF to interact with the iodine anion (Zhao et al., 2019). In this work, the water-stable composite MIL-125- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) chitosan was applied to adsorb iodide from aqueous solution, which is the first time to remove iodide using MOFs@chitosan to the best of our knowledge.
## 2. Material and method
## 2.1. Materials
2-amino terphthalic acid, titanium isopropoxide, Epichlorohydrine and Chitosan (CS) (85 % degree of deacetylated and molecular weight of 150 kDa) were purchased from Alfa Aesar. All solvents and reagents were of analytical class and were used without further purification. Deionized water was used in all experimental processes.
## 2.2. Preparation of cross-linked chitosan film
The chitosan films were prepared using the solvent casting technique. 2 g Chitosan was firstly dissolved in 100 ml (2 %) glacial acetic
acid. The mixture was stirred until a clear solution was obtained. To this solution; \(0.5\,\mathrm{ml}\) of the cross-linker (epichlorohydrine) was added dropwise with continuous stirring in water bath at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for about \(4\,\mathrm{h}\). Afterword \(20\,\mathrm{ml}\) of this solution was poured onto \(15\,\mathrm{cm}\) petridish and kept overnight in oven at \(50\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for complete drying to obtain blank chitosan film.
## 2.3. Preparation of MIL-125- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)
\(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}}\) was prepared according to the literature (Abdelhameed & El-Shahat, 2019; Fan, Zhang, Qin, Li, & Qi, 2018) with some modifications as follow: both \(3.38\,\mathrm{mmol}\) titanium isopropoxide and \(5.5\,\mathrm{mmol}\) 2-aminoterephthalic acid \((5.5\,\mathrm{mmol})\) was dissolved in \(10\,\mathrm{mL}\) dimethylformamide/methanol \((9{:}1\ \mathrm{v/v})\). The mixture was heated in oven at \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(20\,\mathrm{h}\). The formed solids were separated and washed with methanol then dried in oven at \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) to activate and remove methanol from the pores.
2.4. Incorporation of MIL-125- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) onto cross-linked chitosan membrane
Chitosan-MOF Composite films was prepared as post synthetic modification of chitosan membrane. The prepared MIL-125- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) was
added to the cross-linked chitosan membrane with continuous stirring for two hours then sonicated for another two hours in water bath sonication to assure complete dispersing of MIL-125- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) into the polymer solution. The ratio of MIL-125- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) was kept to be \(20\,\%\) from the total solid content of the polymer.
## 2.5. Characterization
FTIR spectroscopy (Mattson 5000 FTIR spectrometer) from \(4000\,-500\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) was selected to verify the cross-linked chitosan, with the comparison of pure chitosan. The size and surface morphology of CS were characterized with a scanning electron microscopy (QUANTA FEG250, USA). The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area was performed by \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption at \(196\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) on an AutosorbiQ-C automated gas sorption system (Quantachrome Instruments, USA). Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA, Mettler-Toledo) was conducted to obtain the thermostability of CS in the air atmosphere flow with a heating rate of \(10\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}\) from room temperature to \(1000\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
## 2.6. Adsorption experiments
## 2.6.1. Batch adsorption studies
The batch adsorption experiments of iodine onto Cross linked chitosan, MIL-125- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and MIL-125- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) chitosan were investigated. The experimental procedures used for determination of kinetics parameters were \(10\,\mathrm{mL}\) solution of iodine at \(400\,\mathrm{mg/L}\) concentration and \(1\,\mathrm{g/L}\) composite film. The solution was placed to the thermostatic shaker at \(150\,\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(90\,\mathrm{min}\) and we took sample every \(10\,\mathrm{min}\) for test the concentration of iodine in the solution. In order to determine the optimal dose, the adsorbent content was ranged from \(0.1{-}2.5\,\mathrm{g/L}\) , on the iodide \(400\,\mathrm{mg/L}\) concentration for \(90\,\mathrm{min}\) adsorption at \(25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (the volume of iodine solutions that were placed in contact with the chitosan membrane is \(1000\,\mathrm{mL}\) ). The adsorption isotherm was calculated as results of mixing different iodine solution concentrations of \(5-20\,\mathrm{mg/L}\) with \(1\,\mathrm{g/L}\) cross linked chitosan, \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH}_{2}@\) chitosan at \(25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(90\,\mathrm{min}\) . The effect of \(\mathsf{p H}\) on the adsorption of iodine onto cross linked chitosan, \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH}_{2}@\) chitosan was studied on range from 4-8. The \(\mathsf{p H}\) of the solution was adjusted into pH 4, 5, 6, 7 and \(\mathsf{p H}\,8\) with \(0.5\,\mathrm{M}\) HCl or \(0.5\,\mathrm{M}\) NaOH solution.
## 2.6.2. Determination of iodide concentration
Iodine concentrations were recorded by a UV–vis spectrophotometer (Shimadzu UV spectrophotometer 2401, Japan) at the maximum absorption wavelength of iodine aqueous solutions \((226\,\mathrm{nm})\) . The adsorbed amount of iodine onto the adsorbent was calculated by a mass balance relationship:
\[
Q_{t}=\frac{(C_{o}-C_{t})V}{m}
\]
where \(\mathrm{C}_{0}\) and \(\mathrm{C_{e}}\) \(\mathrm{(mmol/L)}\) are the initial and final iodine concentrations, V (L) is the volume of the iodine solution, and m (g) is the initial weight of the adsorbent.
## 2.6.3. Experiments of desorption and reusability
In the field of water remediation, the adsorbents must follow the best recyclability and moreover, safety and low price. Here, MIL-125- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\) Cross Linked Chitosan was investigated the desorption and reusability. The \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH}_{2}@\) Cross Linked Chitosan after the batched adsorption were immersed into \(0.5\,\mathrm{M}\) NaCl solution and stirred overnight at room temperature for the desorption. Subsequently, the MIL\(125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}@\) Cross Linked Chitosan was filtrated off to be washed with
deionized water until no iodine was detected by UV–vis spectrophotometer. The regenerated films were then again used to adsorb iodine with the same adsorption conditions. The experiment was repeated five times in order to analyze the recyclable adsorption performance. The concentrations of desorbed iodine in the saline solution and the concentrations of iodine in the wash water were determined to know if in fact all adsorbed iodine was desorbed.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Post synthetic covalent modification of chitosan film
The novel method for modification of chitosan with titanium based metal organic frameworks was shown in Fig. 1a. Chitosan has free amino group can be interact with epichlorohydrine via ring opening of oxirane followed by ring closer with chlorine removal introducing intermediate (epichlorohydrine functionalized chitosan) then the free amino group in MIL-125- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) interact with the intermediate via oxiran ring opening to produce MIL-125-NH2@Cross Linked Chitosan. The covalent bond formation between metal organic framework and chitosan make the composite resist the decomposition in aqueous solution.
## 3.2. Characterization of MIL-125-NH2@chitosan composite membrane
The XRD spectrum of MIL-125-NH2, Cross linked chitosan and MIL\(125\mathrm{-NH_{2}@}\) chitosan composite are located in Fig. 2a. XRD spectra of MIL-125-NH2 show characteristic major peaks at \(6.8^{\circ}\) , \(9.7^{\circ}\) , \(11.6^{\circ}\) , \(15.1^{\circ}\) ,
\(17.9^{\circ}\) and \(19.5^{\circ}\) . Moreover, PXRD of chitosan has two major broad peaks at \(8.7^{\circ}\) and \(20.7^{\circ}\) . However, PXRD of composite shows sharp peaks related to MIL-125-NH2 and peaks realted to chitosan is not appeared due to MOFs material is more crystalline than chitosan.
FTIR spectrum of pure chitosan and modified chitosan with epichlorohydrine was located in Fig. S1. The major bands for the chitosan were appeared as follows (Fernandes Queiroz, Melo, Sabry, Sassaki, & Rocha, 2015): A broad peak appears at \(3356~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) attributing to \(\mathrm{O-H}\) stretching vibration and the peak at \(2864~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) are due to \(\mathsf{C{-}H}\) . The presence of amide group was confirmed by the band at around \(1651\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) \(\scriptstyle{C=0}\) stretching of amide in residual N-acetyl groups of chitosan). Bending vibration bands at 1593, 1421 and \(1376~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) were related to \(\mathrm{N-H}\) , \(\mathrm{CH-OH}\) and \(\mathrm{CH_{2}{-}O H}\) , respectively. Peaks at \(1024\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) this related to \(\mathsf{C{-}O{-}C}\) vibrations of glycosidic bond in chitosan (Abdelhameed, El-Zawahry, & Emam, 2018; Emam, Abdellatif, & Abdelhameed, 2018). The FTIR of modified chitosan with epichlorohydrine shows all characteristics bands of pure chitosan with new vibration bands at 960 and \(926\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) in which related to oxirane ring (Wang & Polavarapu, 2000) in the modified chitosan arm (see Fig. 1a).
Fig. 2b was conducted to observe the transformation of the functional groups in cross linked chitosan during the modification process with MIL-125-NH2 in details. The characteristic peak for MIL-125-NH2 (curve a in Fig. 2) display peaks at 1600, 1500, 1440 and \(1400\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) are belongs to asymmetric and symmetric carbonyl stretching vibrations in MIL-125-NH2, whereas crests at \(1250\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) allocated to the \(\mathsf{C{-}H}\) symmetric extending vibrations of the benzene ring. Moreover, at range \(400-800\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) displays the Ti-O-Ti-O vibrations peaks.
Furthermore, the characteristic peaks at 3448 and \(3334\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) belong to amino group in MIL-125-NH\(_2\) backbone (Fig. S2)(Abdelhameed & ElShahat, 2019; Abdelhameed et al., 2019; Fan et al., 2018). The novel nanocomposite MIL-125-NH\(_2\)\(@\)Chitosan (curve c in Fig. 2) showed new peaks compared to cross-linked chitosan at 1540, 1263, 849, 723 and \(628\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) related to vibrations of the benzene ring and Ti-O-Ti-O. This is confirming the successful incorporation of MOFs in chitosan matrix. Additionally, the peaks at 960 and \(926\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) was disappeared, this is confirm that free amino group in MOFs is reacted with the oxirane ring in cross-linked chitosan arm via ring opening mechanism.
Fig. 2c shows a comparison of the TGA curves of MIL-125-NH\(_2\), Cross linked chitosan, MIL-125-NH\(_2\)\(@\)Chitosan, I\(_2\)\(@\)MIL-125-NH\(_2\), I\(_2\)\(@\)Chitosan and I\(_2\)\(@\)MIL-125-NH\(_2\)\(@\)chitosan. For the MOF test, the 20 % weight loss at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) can be ascribed to the evacuation of the adsorbed dissolvable atoms. The \(60\,\%\) weight loss at almost \(350\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) shows the decay of the MOF structure, which moreover concurs with the exploratory perceptions gotten by others (Hu et al., 2014). For the chitosan test, the \(10\,\%\) weight loss at \(20-200\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) related to water release. The \(70\,\%\) weight loss at \(200-450\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) related to chitosan carbonization (Fig. 2c). For the case of the composite (MIL-125-NH\(_2\)\(@\)chitosan), the TGA result shows that the first stage of weight loss begins at \(370\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), which is larger than that of the MOF sample. This indicates that the combination of MIL-125-NH\(_2\) with cross linked chitosan can slightly enhance the thermal stability of the MOF.
Fig. S3 shows the TGA profile of chitosan, MIL-125-NH\(_2\) and MIL
\(125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}@\) chitosan before and after iodine adsorption. In over all, the thermal decomposition of the \(\mathrm{I}_{2}\) loaded film was faster than its blank film. For I\(_2\)\(@\)chitosan (Fig. S1a), the iodine loaded film started to decompose at \(\sim\!210\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The flash point of iodine is at \(184.3\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Mahdi, Chaudhuri, & Tan, 2016), this increasing in temperature is guessing to be due to the \(\mathrm{I}_{2}\) bonded to the free amino groups of the chitosan. For I\(_2\)\(@\)MIL-125-NH\(_2\) (Fig. S1a), the iodine loaded film decomposes at a slower rate than blank film. This is related to the release of iodine from MIL-125-NH\(_2\) pores is high when MIL-125-NH\(_2\) cages collapse via thermal decomposition. Fig. S1c shows the TGA profiles of MIL-125-NH\(_2\)\(@\)chitosan before and after iodine adsorption. It is seen that the I\(_2\)\(@\)MIL-125-NH\(_2\)\(@\)chitosan has almost the same decomposition profile as MIL-125-NH\(_2\)\(@\)chitosan.
The morphology of cross linked chitosan and MIL-125-NH\(_2\) are located in Fig. 3. Cross linked chitosan membrane show about hexagonal porous surfaces (Fig. 3a,b). Fig. 3c shows the plates of MIL-125-NH\(_2\) with average size \(50\,\mathrm{nm}\). As appeared in Fig. 3d, the EDX of cross linked chitosan appear as it were the component carbon, oxygen and nitrogen. The surface morphology of modified chitosan with MIL-125-NH\(_2\) was observed in Fig. 4(a)–(c). The formed porous structures seem to be equipped with the plates of MIL-125-NH\(_2\) nanoparticles. The good confirmation was obtained from EDX (Fig. 4d), the appearance of titanium metal in the elemental analysis of the composite confirm the successful incorporation of MIL-125-NH\(_2\) onto modified chitosan pore.
## 3.3. Adsorption kinetics and isotherms
The adsorption kinetics explores the relationship between adsorption amount \( \mathrm{Q_{t}} \) and time \( t \). The pseudo-first order model and pseudo-second order model of adsorption were as a rule communicated as Eqs. (2) and (3):
\[
Q_{t}=Q_{e}\left(1-exp^{-k_{1}t}\right)
\]
\[
Q_{t}=\frac{k_{2}Q_{e}^{2}t}{1+k_{2}tQ_{e}}
\]
In Expressions (2) and (3), \( \mathrm{Q_{t}} \) \( \mathrm{(mmol/g)} \) and \( \mathrm{Qe} \) \( \mathrm{(mmol/g)} \) were adsorption amount at \( t \) moment and at equilibrium, with the rate constants of \( \mathrm{K_{1}} \) as first-order \( (\mathrm{mm}^{-1}) \) and \( \mathrm{K_{2}} \) as second-order \( (\mathrm{g} \) \( \mathrm{mmol}^{-1}\,\mathrm{min}^{-1}) \). The calculated parameters along with the correlation coefficient \( (\mathbf{R}^{2}) \) of the two models were listed in Table 1.
The effect of time on the adsorption of iodine onto chitosan, MIL\(125\mathrm{-NH_{2}}\) , MIL- \(125\mathrm{-NH_{2}@}\) chitosan has been shown in Fig. 5. The best time for adsorption equilibrium is \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) . At that time, the adsorption efficiency is \(35~\%\) , \(85~\%\) and \(89~\%\) when using chitosan, \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}}\) , \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}@}\) chitosan, respectively. Unexpected results were shown in the improvement of the efficiency of iodine adsorption onto MIL-125- \(\mathrm{NH_{2}@}\) chitosan. The efficiency of modified composite film is 2.54 times more than cross-linked chitosan. At the end of experiment (after \(90\,\mathrm{min})\) , the adsorption efficiency is \(54\;\%\) , \(90~\%\) and \(96\;\%\) for chitosan, \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}}\) , \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}@}\) chitosan, respectively. When comparing the prepared MOFs \((\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}})\) with the composite film (MIL-125- \(\mathrm{NH_{2}@}\) chitosan), the results show a little improvement in efficiency (0.07 times). However, chitosan is not only act as an immobilizing membrane but also display high adsorption capacity. Because MIL-125- \(\mathrm{NH_{2}@}\) chitosan film has only \(20\,\%\,\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}}\) content, the adsorption efficiency of the \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}}\) content in \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}@}\) chitosan film is only \(18~\%\) , so chitosan in composite have \(78~\%\) enhancement in the adsorption efficiency. These confirm that both chitosan and MOFs play vital role in the adsorption of iodine. The pseudo-second-order adsorption kinetics model is more suitable for our data fitting \((\mathbf{R}^{2}>0.97)\) . However, the adsorption rate in the presence of composite film was higher than MOFs and cross-linked chitosan with about 1.7 times and 4.3 times, respectively.
Fig. 6 show the adsorption isotherm for the adsorption of iodine onto cross-linked chitosan, \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}}\) and \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}@}\) crosslinked chitosan. The most important results which obtained from this figure are the fitting of the data with Langmuir model. Moreover; the adsorption capacity was 216.63, 393.12 and \(399.68\,\mathrm{mg/g}\) when using cross-linked chitosan, \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}}\) and \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}@}\) cross-linked chitosan, respectively (Table 2). From these results we found that MIL\(125\mathrm{-NH_{2}@}\) cross-linked chitosan film has adsorption capacity higher than cross-linked chitosan with about \(84.7~\%\) . This is may be related to the presence of MOFs in the frame of cross-linked chitosan film. Comparing the present adsorption capacity of \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}@}\) cross-linked chitosan with recently reported adsorbents of chitosan (montmormontite coating onto crosslinked chitosan has adsorption capacity \(38.48\,\mathrm{mg/g}\) (Li et al., 2019) and chitosan microspheres have adsorption capacity \(111.57\,\mathrm{mg/g}\) (Zhang et al., 2019)), which confirm that MIL\(125\mathrm{-NH_{2}@}\) cross-linked chitosan is the best.
## 3.4. Effect of adsorbent dosage, pH value and reusability
Searching about optimal dose is more important in adsorption process, so here in this work different amount of prepared materials (cross linked chitosan and \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}@}\) chitosan composite film) were dispersed into iodide solution to investigate iodine adsorption performance. With the increase of adsorbent dosage, the active sites for adsorption increased, thus, more iodide ions were adsorbed on the film, seeing in Fig. 7a, whereas, the adsorptive amount of iodide ions reached the plateau because of the equilibrium when the dosage was up to \(1\,\mathrm{g}/\)
L. It was shown from Fig. 7a that: when the adsorbent dose was \(0.5\,\mathrm{g/L}\) ; the removal percentages of iodine were \(38\;\%\) , \(63\;\%\) and \(85\;\%\) , for cross linked chitosan, \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}}\) , and \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}@}\) chitosan composite film, respectively. \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}@}\) chitosan composite film show higher capacity than chitosan by 2.2 times, this confirms the useful of the modification. At the plateau \((1\,\mathrm{g/L})\) , the removal percentage was \(47\;\%\) , \(81\ \%\) and \(93\ \%\) for cross linked chitosan and \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}@}\) chitosan composite film, respectively. The rate of removal at the high dose concentration was slow may be related to remove of most iodine in the solution in the case of \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}@}\) chitosan, but in the case of chitosan, the slow removal rate may be due to the interaction between the chitosan film with each other.
Because of the importance of \(\mathsf{p H}\) in the adsorption process (changing the surface charge of adsorbent), the iodine uptake was investigated with different \(\mathsf{p H}\) values (Fig. 7b). The iodine adsorption capacities of cross linked chitosan were 247, 230, 224, 209 and \(189\,\mathrm{mg/g}\) at \(\mathrm{pH}\,4,\,5,\,6,\,7\) and \(\mathsf{p H}\,8\) ; respectively. It is shown that by increasing the \(\mathsf{p H}\) values the adsorption capacity decreased. In acidic medium we found that the adsorption capacity is higher than basic medium, it may be due to the protonation of chitosan which facilitate the iodine adsorption by charge-charge interaction mechanism. While in basic medium the adsorption performance was weak due to the competitive adsorption of hydroxyl ion in the solution (Mariappan, Vairamuthu, & Ganapathy, 2015). The iodine adsorption capacities of \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}@}\) chitosan were 345, 400, 419, 370 and \(278\,\mathrm{mg/g}\) at \(\mathsf{p H}\) 4, 5, 6, 7 and \(\mathsf{p H}\,8\) ; respectively. It is found that by increase \(\mathsf{p H}\) values the iodine adsorption capacity increase until \(\mathsf{p H}\,6\) then decreased in higher pH values. \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}@}\) chitosan has high adsorption capacity of \(\mathsf{p H}\) near the neutral conditions \(\left(\mathsf{p H}\ 6\right)\) , which it is useful for test adsorption capacities of the absorbers.
confirming that the best performance for capture of iodine (Table 2).
## 3.5. Adsorption mechanism
The behavior of iodine uptake onto \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}@}\) cross-linked chitosan was briefly explained in Fig. 1b. The \(-\mathrm{NH}-\) of the composite can pronated into \(-\mathrm{NH_{2}}^{+}\) - then adsorb the \(\mathrm{I}^{-}\) ions through the electrostatic attraction. Another supposed mechanism is chemisorption of iodine onto \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}@}\) cross-linked chitosan via the host–guest interaction. Iodine can adsorbed via the weak interaction between \(\mathrm{I}_{2}\) and the benzene ring on the backbone of MOFs. The other corresponding to the amino group in the MOFs structure can acts as a molecular trap for iodine owing to its strong affinity for this molecule and transfers electrons to iodine in the large conjugated ring. To further understand the mechanism of iodine adsorption, we recorded the EDX of \(\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}@}\) cross-linked chitosan (Fig. 1b). The appearance of the iodine peak confirmed that iodine was adsorbed onto the materials.
## 4. Conclusion
Chitosan film was modified via cross-linked protocol with epichlorohydrine then post covalent modified with \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}}\) . The obtained \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}@}\) cross-linked chitosan films were investigated and proposed to be as the effective adsorbent for iodine removal. The film was characterized with scan electron microscope, BET surface area and PXRD. The maximum adsorption capacity of iodine was 216.63, 393.12 and \(399.68\,\mathrm{mg/g}\) by cross-linked chitosan films, \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}}\) and \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}@}\) cross-linked chitosan films, respectively. Moreover, well-fitting with Langmuir model was observed and the kinetic data fit best in the pseudo-second order model of adsorption. Nevertheless, \(\mathrm{MIL-125-NH_{2}@}\) cross-linked chitosan films could be the prospective way for the iodine removal in the wastes, with the efficient adsorption performance and the remarkable stability represented in five cycles.
